    "headingid": 544,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70464,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 544,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Testis</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:53:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 506,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 545,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70465,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 545,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The usual presentation is a testicular mass; very rarely, there is gynaecomastia or premature puberty {32719989}. JGCTs are occasionally associated with karyotypic abnormalities {27626837; 25440720} or cryptorchidism {32719989}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:53:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 507,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 546,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70466,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 546,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>JGCTs predominantly occur in the first 6&nbsp;months of life, and very rarely beyond the first year {32719989} or in adults {17406141; 18313118}. They account for 6&ndash;7% of all prepubertal tumours of the testis and &lt;&nbsp;0.5% of all sex cord stromal tumours of the testis {3976984; 9134041; 20592370; 20850634; 32719989}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:53:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 508,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 547,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70467,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 547,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:53:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 509,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 548,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70468,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 548,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:53:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 516,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 555,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70469,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 555,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The median tumour size is 20&nbsp;mm (range: 5&ndash;50&nbsp;mm). The cut surface is often partly cystic, containing watery or mucoid fluid, with tan-white/yellow solid areas {32719989; 26076062; 3976984}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:53:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 510,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 549,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70470,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 549,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A lobulated growth pattern with macrocystic and microcystic areas is characteristic. The cysts are filled with eosinophilic or basophilic fluid. A solid component is also usual; papillary, basaloid, and spindle cell patterns rarely occur. The tumour cells are polygonal with vacuolated or eosinophilic cytoplasm; they have ovoid nuclei without grooves and with small nucleoli. Fibrous or fibromyxoid stroma is frequent. Mitotic activity (&gt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;mitoses/mm<sup>2</sup>) and apoptosis may be prominent. Regressive changes (hyalinization) may be seen. Rete testis involvement, entrapment of seminiferous tubules, and lymphovascular invasion can occur {26076062; 3976984}.</p>\r\n<p>JGCTs commonly express SF1, calretinin, inhibin, SOX9, WT1, and FOXL2, as well as (focally) low-molecular-weight cytokeratins {26076062; 18721930}.</p>\r\n<p>Differential diagnoses include yolk sac tumour (YST) and other sex cord stromal tumours. The nuclear features distinguish the JGCT from the adult granulosa cell tumour. Unlike JGCTs, only one&nbsp;fifth of prepubertal YSTs arise in patients aged &lt;&nbsp;1&nbsp;year {25828390}. Microscopically, follicular elements are not a feature of YST. Immunohistochemistry is usually straightforward; unlike YSTs, JGCTs are negative for SALL4, AFP, and glypican-3 (GPC3) {26076062}. The differentiation from other sex cord stromal tumours may be difficult, but the presence of follicles and a younger age favour a diagnosis of JGCT.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:53:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 511,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 550,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70471,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 550,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There are no reports in the literature.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:53:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 512,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 551,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70472,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 551,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>FOXL2</em> mutations have not yet been systematically investigated; one investigated case was <em>FOXL2</em>-wildtype {22742556; 19516027}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:53:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 515,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 554,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 70473,
        "bookId": 36,
        "chapterId": 150,
        "headingId": 554,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> a partially macrocystic or microcystic tumour composed of primitive granulosa cells; diffuse or lobular growth; follicles of variable size and shape.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> manifestation in neonates and infants in the first year of life; typical sex cord stromal tumour immunophenotype.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2022-02-22T11:53:11"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 513,
    "bookid": 36,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
